    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All tests pass \u2014 **62 run, 0 failures, 0 errors, BUILD SUCCESS**, including the new `Cp50Tests` idempotency test.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented idempotent owner creation in `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`, the single `POST /api/owners` handler.\n\n**How it works:**\n1. **Read the key** \u2014 a helper `currentIdempotencyKey()` reads the `Idempotency-Key` header from the bound servlet request via `RequestContextHolder` (the generated `OwnersApi` interface signature has no header parameter, so this is the clean way to access it without regenerating the API).\n2. **Short-circuit repeats** \u2014 at the very start of `addOwner` (before any validation or duplicate/identity checks that would otherwise 409), if the key has been seen the controller re-fetches the originally created owner and returns it with **200 OK**.\n3. **Record on success** \u2014 after a successful `saveOwner`, the key is stored mapping to the new owner's id in an in-memory `ConcurrentHashMap`.\n\nSo a first create returns 201 (Created) as before; a repeat with the same key returns 200 with the same owner id, instead of creating a duplicate.\n\nOne design note worth flagging: the idempotency store is an in-memory map on the controller bean. That satisfies the spec and tests and is correct for a single instance, but it is not shared across multiple app instances and is not persisted across restarts. If you need cross-instance/durable idempotency, that store would need to move to the database or a shared cache \u2014 happy to do that if it's in scope.",
